# Flutter/Dart coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Dart
(3.x, sound null safety — mandatory since Dart 3, it cannot be disabled)
and Flutter widget idiom. Applies only to `*.dart` files — everything not
Dart/Flutter-specific still comes from the common rules this file
`extends`.

## Naming and structure

- `UpperCamelCase` for classes, enums, extensions, typedefs, and widgets;
  `lowerCamelCase` for variables, parameters, and methods;
  `lowercase_with_underscores` for file names and package/directory names —
  never `snake_case` identifiers or `PascalCase` file names.
- A widget file is named after the widget it defines
  (`order_summary_card.dart` for `OrderSummaryCard`), one primary public
  widget per file, matching how the project already splits `lib/`.
- Keep a `build()` method reading top-to-bottom as layout, not a mix of
  layout and business logic — push data fetching, formatting, and
  validation into a controller/view-model/state object the widget reads
  from, not inline inside `build()`.
- An identifier's name shrinks with its scope: a short-lived loop index is
  `i`, a public widget or service class gets a full descriptive name.

## Const constructors and rebuild cost

- Mark every widget constructor `const` when all of its fields can be
  `final` and const-constructible — a `const` widget is skipped entirely
  by `build()` on a parent rebuild instead of being rebuilt and diffed,
  which is a real, measurable performance difference in a widget tree
  that rebuilds often (a list item, an animated ancestor).
- Prefer `const` at the call site too (`const SizedBox(height: 8)`, `const
  Divider()`), not just on the constructor declaration — the analyzer's
  `prefer_const_constructors` lint exists to catch the call sites that
  could be const but are not; do not disable that lint to avoid fixing
  them.
- A widget with a non-const field (a callback closure built fresh each
  `build()`, for instance) cannot be const — when that field does not
  actually need to vary per-build, hoist it out (a `static` handler, a
  field on the enclosing `State`) so the widget itself can go back to
  being const.

## Sound null safety idiom

- Prefer `?.`/`??`/`??=` over the bang operator (`!`) — reach for `!` only
  when the value's non-nullability is guaranteed by code the analyzer
  cannot see (already checked a few lines above with no intervening
  `await`, or a documented invariant), and say why in a comment when it is
  not obvious.
- Never use `!` on a value that crossed an `await` since it was last
  checked, or on a field read from a widget/state that could have been
  disposed in between — re-check (`if (value == null) return;` or
  `context.mounted`) after the async gap instead of re-asserting non-null.
- Declare a field `late` only when it is genuinely always initialized
  before first read (e.g. in `initState`) — a `late` field that can
  legitimately be unset just trades a compile-time nullability warning for
  a runtime `LateInitializationError`, which is strictly worse.
- Give a nullable parameter a sensible default via `??` at the point of
  use rather than threading a null check through every downstream
  consumer of that parameter.

## Composition over deep nesting

- Extract a widget into its own class (or a private `_SectionWidget`
  below the file's main widget) once a single `build()` method's nesting
  becomes hard to scan — composition of small, focused widgets is the
  idiomatic Flutter equivalent of extracting a function, and each
  extracted piece can independently be `const` and independently testable
  with `testWidgets`.
- Prefer a `Column`/`Row`/`Wrap` plus a handful of small child widgets
  over one large widget expression with many positional/named-argument
  levels of `Padding(child: Container(child: Column(children: [...])))` —
  flatten with `SizedBox`/`Padding` siblings and extracted widgets instead
  of nesting purely to achieve a visual effect a flatter tree would give
  just as well.
- A `build()` method that branches on more than two or three conditions to
  decide what to render is a sign the widget is doing too much — extract
  each branch into its own small widget so each one is readable and
  testable in isolation.

## Formatting and imports

- Format with `dart format` before finishing a change — do not hand-format
  around a formatter that is already configured; CI enforces `dart format
  --set-exit-if-changed .` in most Flutter repos.
- Order imports `dart:` / `package:flutter` / other `package:` / relative,
  with a blank line between groups, matching whatever the project's
  `analysis_options.yaml` (e.g. `directives_ordering`) already enforces.
- Prefer relative imports within the same package's `lib/` tree and
  `package:` imports for anything outside it, matching the project's
  existing convention rather than switching styles mid-file.
